%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C17F2B17] ## Arrays
%[text] Arrays are the most [fundamental representation](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matrices-and-arrays.html) of data in MATLAB. This is an excellent choice whenever all the data you plan to work with is the same type. Arrays were introduced in the [Fundamentals of Programming](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/103225-fundamentals-of-programming) module, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A 4x5 matrix with 4 rows and 5 columns. There are reference indices for each entry in the form (row,column) such that (3,2) is the cell in the third row and second column](text:image:109a) ![An array represented as three 4x5 matrices where the front one is red, the middle one is green and the rear one is blue. The depth is tracked by a third parameter (row, column, channel)](text:image:5b0a) 
%[text] and are discussed in [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted) regarding the [creation](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted#module=4) of arrays, [getting information in and out](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted#module=5) of arrays, and [performing calculations](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted#module=6) on arrays. You can explore even more in the self-paced online course [MATLAB Fundamentals](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-fundamentals/mlbe).
%[text] ![](text:image:2336) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. Create a `3x5` array named `M` containing [random](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.rand.html) numbers.
%[text] 2. Create a `2x2` array named `S` containing the strings `"This"`, `"is"`, `"an"`, and `"array"`.
%[text] 3. Try to create a `2x2` array named `C` containing the character arrays `'This '`, `'is'`, `'an'` and `'array'`. What happens? What happens if you use `'This'` instead of `'This '?`
%[text] 4. Create a `3x1` array named `V` containing an integer, say `int8(3)`, and two floating point numbers such as `4.7` or `pi`. What happens? Does it matter what order you put the numbers in?
%[text] 5. Create an array  \
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] T = ['Testing,'; 'Testing,'; 32 32 46 46 46 49 50 51]
%[text] ```
%[text]             What happens? Can you use the documentation page for `char` to figure out what is going on? 
% Do your experimenting here

%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3BF96A3B] ### Using Logical Indexing with Arrays
% Set up matrices to use in examples
M = rand(5);
S = ["This" "is" "not" "a" "sentence" "."];
%[text] When you already know which values you want, it is easy to access a particular element like
S(1,1)
%[text] or
S(end)
%[text] Frequently you may not know which indices correspond to the elements you want, however. You can use logical indexing to identify values according to any logical test:
% Print the values in the random matrix M
M
% Create a logical matrix where the (i,j)th entry is true if M(i,j)>0.5
% and false if M(i,j) <= 0.5
M > 0.5
% Reset all the values where M>0.5 to 0
M(M>0.5) = 0 
%[text]  ![](text:image:3334) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. Reset all the values where `M==0` to `16`.
%[text] 2. Reset all the values in `S` which contain an `"s"` to be `"box"`. \

%%
%[text] ![](text:image:8ff9) **Application: Sea Surface Temperatures.**
%[text] Explore the data file:
ncdisp("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900117_C20160820.nc") %[text:anchor:M_D61C3B63]
%[text] ![](text:image:9df5) **Exercise 1**.
%[text] a) What data type is the variable `lat` stored in?
%[text] 
%[text] b)  What are the units of `sea_surface_temperature`?
%[text] 
%[text] c) What value is listed for a missing value of `sea_surface_temperature`?
%[text] 
%[text] d) How is time stored?
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_80389986] #### Load the Sea Surface Temperature Data
%[text] Using the metadata that we just explored, use `ncread` to load the data that is included in the data files for the 17th of each month in 1990, e.g., January 17, 1990, February 17, 1990, .... 
sstData = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900117_C20160820.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData2 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900217_C20160820.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData3 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900317_C20160819.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData4 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900417_C20160819.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData5 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900517_C20160819.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData6 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900617_C20160820.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData7 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900717_C20160820.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData8 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900817_C20160820.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData9 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900917_C20160819.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData10 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19901017_C20160819.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData11 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19901117_C20160819.nc","sea_surface_temperature");
sstData12 = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19901217_C20160820.nc","sea_surface_temperature");

sstAll = cat(3,sstData,sstData2,sstData3,sstData4,sstData5,sstData6,sstData7,...
    sstData8,sstData9,sstData10,sstData11,sstData12);

clear sstData*

% The times, latitude data, and longitude data are identical in each file
tData = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900117_C20160820.nc","time");
latData = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900117_C20160820.nc","lat");
lonData = ncread("SEAFLUX-OSB-CDR_V02R00_SST_D19900117_C20160820.nc","lon");

%%
%[text]  ![](text:image:53ab) **Try**. Visualize the sea surface temperatures as a scaled image. Because you are currently exploring the data, try looking at just the first time slice:
imagesc(sstAll(:,:,1))
%[text] ![](text:image:7475) **Reflect.** 
%[text] 1. Does this image look like a familiar map of the world? Why or why not? \
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3249) **Try**. Recognizing that the data doesn't initially match the shape that you probably expect, try using `pagetranspose` to transpose just each time slice individually. Does that help your results?
sstImage = pagetranspose(sstAll);
imagesc(sstImage(:,:,1))
%[text] Not quite. To present a standard image of the world where north is up, you need one more transformation to flip the image up/down. This can be done using `flipud.` 
sstImage = flipud(pagetranspose(sstAll));

imagesc(sstImage(:,:,1))
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:9a97) **Exercise 1, continued**
%[text] e) Using `datetime` and `years`, `days`, `hours`, `seconds`, etc, create a vector of the dates and times in `tData`. Save this vector as the variable `tJan`.
%[text] ![](text:image:7e46) **Pro-tip**. Remember that you can use a documentation search to find command syntax:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] doc datetime
%[text] ```
% Set tJan to be midnight on Jan 1, 2022
tJan = datetime([2022 01 01 00 00 00]);
%%
%[text] Observe that the `tJan` vector can be modified to create a vector `tAll` by making additional copies of `tJan` with updated month values:
tAll = repmat(tJan,1,12); %[text:anchor:M_5ADABD11]
for m = 2:12
    tAll(:,m).Month = m;
end
tAll = reshape(tAll,[],1);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6121D943] #### Plot the Sea Surface Temperature Data
% figure
tempMax = max(sstImage,[],"all");
tempMin = min(sstImage,[],"all");
scalingFactor = 235/(tempMax-tempMin);

sstImageScaled = (sstImage-tempMin)*scalingFactor+10;
sstImageScaled(isnan(sstImage)) = 140;
load coastlines
coastShifted = [mod((360+coastlon),360)*4 360-coastlat*4];
for k = 1:length(tAll)
    image(sstImageScaled(:,:,k))
    hold on
    plot(coastShifted(:,1), coastShifted(:,2),".","MarkerSize",3)
    hold off
    title("Ocean Surface Temperature, "+string(tAll(k)))
    pause(0.2)
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9E2C53F2] ### Using Linear Indexing with Arrays
%[text] Although arrays are conceptually multi-dimensional, knowing something about how an array is stored in memory can offer additional possibilities for indexing. Since MATLAB is [column-major](file:'./Memory.m':H_26D0062A) by default, a single value indexing into an array is treated as a simple memory offset calculation into the linearly-stored array. 
% Create a 4x4 magic square
M = magic(4)
% Select and display the 1st element of the 3rd column
% which is the 2*4+1 = 9th linearized element
M(9)
% Linearize and display the matrix M
M(:)
%[text] ![](text:image:8aff) **Try**. 
%[text] Before reshaping `tAll`, it was an `8x12` matrix of distinct elements:
tAll = repmat(tJan,1,12);
for m = 2:12
    tAll(:,m).Month = m;
end
size(tAll)
%[text] 1. Determine the linear indices necessary to extract all the times on April 17, 1990 from this form of `tAll`. What is a reasonable name for the new vector?
%[text] 2. What happens if you make the same call after reshaping `tAll`, as [above](internal:M_5ADABD11), into a column vector? \

%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A391D75B] ## Tables
%[text] A table is a collection of tabular data where each row contains one entry in each column-oriented variable. 
%[text] ![](text:image:2870) **Application: Working with Timestamped Numerical and Categorical Data.**
%[text] First, read in the tabular data from the comma-separated value file: `WkOfSolarPowerGeneration.csv`.
solar = readtable("WkOfSolarPowerGeneration.csv","DateLocale","en_US") %[text:anchor:M_B24956CB]
%[text] ![](text:image:36b5) **Try**.  Try some other [`DateLocale` values](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/readmatrix.html?searchHighlight=datelocale&s_tid=srchtitle_datelocale_1#namevaluepairarguments). 
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] solar = readtable("WkOfSolarPowerGeneration.csv","DateLocale","ko_KR")
%[text] solar = readtable("WkOfSolarPowerGeneration.csv","DateLocale","de_DE")
%[text] solar = readtable("WkOfSolarPowerGeneration.csv","DateLocale","ja_JP")
%[text] solar = readtable("WkOfSolarPowerGeneration.csv","DateLocale","es_ES")
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:34ab) \*\*Reflect. \*\*Why do you think the `readtable` function allows you to specify the `DateLocale` parameter? 
%[text] When loaded properly, the first column is a `datetime` and the second column is a `double`, but what is the third column of data?
class(solar.Sun_up)
%[text] Apparently, `readtable` couldn't identify the data type of the `Sun_up` column, so it has been pulled into MATLAB as the general cell array data type. However, we can fix that by supplying additional information. By quick inspection, the only values that `Sun_up` takes are `"yes"` and `"no"`. It should be a categorical data type:
solar.Sun_up = categorical(solar.Sun_up)
class(solar.Sun_up)
%[text] One useful feature of tables is the `summary` command which attempts to provide a summary of the information in the table. The type of summary will depend on the data type of that column. 
summary(solar)
%[text] Let's investigate these missing values. 
idx = find(ismissing(solar.AC_kw))
%[text] Look at the values near the missing value:
solar.AC_kw(idx-3:idx+3)
%[text] In this case it seems very legitimate to fill in this missing data with a copy of the previous value:
solar.AC_kw(idx) = solar.AC_kw(idx-1);
%[text] ![](text:image:7123) **Exercise 2**.
%[text] Decide how to account for the missing values in `solar.Sun_up`. Does it make the most sense to use `fillmissing` or `rmmissing` in this case? 

%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7709) **Exercise 3**.
%[text] a) How often were the solar power arrays generating power when the sun was down? What was the total power generated while the sun was down?

%[text] b) On which days were the solar power arrays not generating power when the sun was up?

%[text] c) What fraction of the time were the solar power arrays not generating power when the sun was up?

